---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import ReproductionSim from '../../simulators/reproduction/ReproductionSim.astro';
import { reproductionQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/reproduction';

const toc = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'What the reproductive system does' },
  { id: 'female', label: 'Female reproductive anatomy' },
  { id: 'male', label: 'Male reproductive anatomy' },
  { id: 'gametes', label: 'Gamete production' },
  { id: 'hormones', label: 'Reproductive hormones' },
  { id: 'cycle', label: 'The menstrual cycle' },
  { id: 'fertilisation', label: 'Fertilisation and early development' },
  { id: 'relevance', label: 'Health and medicine' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Chapter 27: The Reproductive System. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/27-introduction' },
  { text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Section 28.2: Embryonic Development. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development' },
  { text: 'Moore, K. L., Persaud, T. V. N., & Torchia, M. G. (2019). The Developing Human: Clinically Oriented Embryology (11th ed.). Elsevier.' },
  { text: 'Reed, B. G., & Carr, B. R. (2018). The Normal Menstrual Cycle and the Control of Ovulation. In Endotext. MDText.com.', url: 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279054/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="anatomy-physiology"
  topic="reproductive-system"
  description="Interactive human reproductive system simulator: explore labelled diagrams of the female and male systems, follow sperm and egg transport, step through the menstrual cycle hormones and watch early embryo development."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={reproductionQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Reproduction is how every living thing passes on its genes. In humans, two specialised systems produce sex cells
      (gametes), and a carefully timed set of hormones prepares the body for a possible pregnancy. Explore the anatomy,
      step through the monthly cycle, and follow the first ten days after fertilisation.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <ReproductionSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>view</strong>. In the anatomy views, <strong>click any structure</strong> to learn its function, and compare the magnified egg and sperm cells.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Menstrual cycle</strong>, press <strong>Start</strong> or drag the <strong>day</strong> slider and watch the hormones, the ovary and the uterine lining change together.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>A fertilised egg implants</strong> to see how hCG and progesterone keep the lining and stop menstruation.</li>
      <li>In <strong>Fertilisation</strong>, press <strong>Start</strong> to follow the early embryo from fertilisation in the tube to implantation in the uterus.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="overview">What the reproductive system does</h2>
  <p>
    The reproductive systems produce <strong>gametes</strong> (eggs and sperm), make <strong>sex hormones</strong> that
    control development and fertility, and, in females, support a developing baby. Unlike other body systems, they are
    not needed for an individual to survive, but they are essential for the species. They become fully active at
    <strong>puberty</strong>, when rising hormone levels cause the body to mature.
  </p>

  <h2 id="female">Female reproductive anatomy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ovaries:</strong> two almond-sized glands that store immature eggs and release one, usually each month. They also make oestrogen and progesterone.</li>
    <li><strong>Fallopian tubes:</strong> carry the egg from the ovary to the uterus; fertilisation usually happens here.</li>
    <li><strong>Uterus:</strong> a muscular organ with a lining (the endometrium) where an embryo can implant and grow.</li>
    <li><strong>Cervix:</strong> the neck of the uterus, opening into the vagina.</li>
    <li><strong>Vagina:</strong> a muscular canal from the cervix to the outside of the body, also the birth canal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="male">Male reproductive anatomy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Testes:</strong> make sperm and testosterone. They sit outside the abdomen in the <strong>scrotum</strong>, which keeps them slightly cooler, as sperm production needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Epididymis:</strong> a long coiled tube where sperm mature and are stored.</li>
    <li><strong>Vas deferens (sperm ducts):</strong> carry sperm towards the urethra.</li>
    <li><strong>Seminal vesicles and prostate gland:</strong> add fluids that provide energy and protect sperm, forming semen.</li>
    <li><strong>Urethra:</strong> carries urine or semen out of the body through the penis.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gametes">Gamete production</h2>
  <p>
    Gametes are made by <strong>meiosis</strong>, a type of cell division that halves the chromosome number from 46 to 23.
    Sperm production (<strong>spermatogenesis</strong>) begins at puberty and continues throughout life, producing millions
    of sperm a day; each takes about 74 days to form. Egg production (<strong>oogenesis</strong>) starts before birth: a
    baby girl is born with all her immature eggs, and from puberty one usually matures each month until
    <strong>menopause</strong>. Each gamete is adapted to its job: the sperm has a tail and many mitochondria; the egg is
    large with a food store.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hormones">Reproductive hormones</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>hypothalamus</strong> and <strong>pituitary gland</strong> in the brain control reproduction. The pituitary
    releases <strong>FSH</strong> (follicle-stimulating hormone) and <strong>LH</strong> (luteinising hormone), which act on
    the ovaries and testes. The ovaries respond by making <strong>oestrogen</strong> and <strong>progesterone</strong>; the
    testes make <strong>testosterone</strong>. These sex hormones cause the changes of puberty and control the menstrual
    cycle and sperm production, often through feedback loops.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cycle">The menstrual cycle</h2>
  <p>
    The cycle lasts about 28 days on average, but anywhere from about 21 to 35 days is common. During the
    <strong>follicular phase</strong>, FSH makes a follicle grow and oestrogen rebuilds the uterine lining. A surge of LH
    triggers <strong>ovulation</strong>, the release of an egg, around the middle of the cycle. In the
    <strong>luteal phase</strong>, the empty follicle becomes the <strong>corpus luteum</strong>, which makes progesterone
    to keep the lining ready. If there is no pregnancy, hormone levels fall and the lining is shed during
    <strong>menstruation</strong>, a period, which marks day 1 of the next cycle.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fertilisation">Fertilisation and early development</h2>
  <p>
    If a sperm meets the egg in a fallopian tube, enzymes in the sperm’s acrosome help it through the egg’s outer layer.
    Once one sperm enters, the egg’s coat changes to block others. The nuclei combine to form a <strong>zygote</strong>,
    which divides repeatedly as it travels to the uterus, becoming a ball of cells (morula) and then a hollow
    <strong>blastocyst</strong>. About six to ten days after fertilisation it implants in the uterine lining and begins to
    make <strong>hCG</strong>, the hormone detected by pregnancy tests. Over the following weeks the placenta forms and the
    embryo’s organs begin to develop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="relevance">Health and medicine</h2>
  <p>
    Understanding the reproductive system underpins fertility treatments such as IVF (in vitro fertilisation),
    contraception, which works by preventing ovulation, fertilisation or implantation, and screening for conditions such
    as testicular and cervical cancer. Hormone levels such as FSH and LH are measured to investigate fertility and
    puberty. Talking to a doctor or nurse is the right step for any personal health question.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Educational model only.</strong> The diagrams are simplified schematics, and the cycle shows an idealised
      28-day pattern with relative hormone levels; real cycles vary widely between people and from month to month. This
      simulator is not medical advice and cannot be used to track fertility or plan or prevent pregnancy. See our
      <a href="/disclaimer/">educational and medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Gamete</dt>
    <dd>A sex cell (egg or sperm) containing 23 chromosomes.</dd>
    <dt>Ovulation</dt>
    <dd>The release of an egg from an ovary.</dd>
    <dt>Zygote</dt>
    <dd>The single cell formed when an egg and sperm fuse.</dd>
    <dt>Implantation</dt>
    <dd>The embedding of the early embryo in the uterine lining.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Testes make sperm and testosterone; ovaries release eggs and make oestrogen and progesterone.</li>
    <li>Gametes are made by meiosis and carry 23 chromosomes.</li>
    <li>FSH, LH, oestrogen and progesterone coordinate the menstrual cycle, with ovulation around mid-cycle.</li>
    <li>Fertilisation happens in a fallopian tube; the embryo implants in the uterus about a week later.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: hormone curves show relative levels for an idealised cycle; units and timings differ between people.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
